Now I understand the parsing logic. Let me analyze the potential heap-buffer-overflow READ 1.

In `g_date_fill_parse_tokens`:
```c
s = (const guchar *) str;
pt->num_ints = 0;
while (*s && pt->num_ints < 4)
  {
    i = 0;
    while (*s && g_ascii_isdigit (*s) && i < NUM_LEN)
      {
        num[pt->num_ints][i] = *s;
        ++s;
        ++i;
      }
    ...
```

The `num` array is `gchar num[4][NUM_LEN+1]` = `num[4][11]`. The inner loop writes `num[pt->num_ints][i]` where `i < NUM_LEN` (i.e., i < 10). So it writes indices 0..9 into `num[pt->num_ints]` which has 11 elements. That's fine.

Wait, but `pt->num_ints` can be up to 3 (since the outer loop stops when `pt->num_ints < 4`). So `num[pt->num_ints]` accesses num[0], num[1], num[2], num[3]. That's fine, 4 rows.

